  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 445 AM EDT MON AUG 3 2026\n\nThe first part of the extended will be a continuation of damp \nweather with a threat for thunderstorms and excessive rainfall\nlingering through Thursday. The diurnal cycle should dominate the\ntiming of the convection each day. Later, broad troughing looks to\nbe on track to spread south from Canada into the Ohio Valley\nclosing out the work week and heading into the weekend - perhaps\nbringing more shower and thunderstorm chances - continuing the \ndamp pattern that started this month.\n\nThe previous long term discussion follows:\n\nThe models remain in good agreement with the long wave pattern \nthrough the end of next week. An upper level ridge will remain \ncentered across the western Atlantic, strengthening through the \nlatter half of the work week. Further west, a persistent heat dome\ncentered near the Four Corners region will also intensify during \nthe period. Meanwhile, a deeper upper level low spiraling over the\nvicinity of Hudson Bay will spread cyclonic flow over the north \ncentral U.S. through next week. By the start of next weekend, both\nridges will have dampened somewhat, with more model uncertainty \nregarding how much residual troughing can be maintained across the\nTennessee Valley and portions of the Deep South. In general, this\nwill result in a continuation of diurnally-driven convection each\nday through next week across eastern Kentucky, with elevated \nhumidity levels and near normal temperatures expected for early \nAugust. \n\nThe models have trended more towards sustaining a closed low over \nthe Deep South on Tuesday, with higher PoPs favored across our\nterrain, and lesser chances north, further away from the forcing.\nThe upper low will drift closer to us on Wednesday, bringing\nhigher rain chances to eastern Kentucky. LREF mean PWATs continue\nto range in the 50-75th percentile range of climatology, but storm\nmotions look a bit slower this go around at around 10 kts, so\nlocally heavy rainfall will be possible. The upper level low will\ndampen across the region on Thursday, with some degree of \ntroughing remaining in place through the weekend, dependent on the\nstrength of upstream cyclonic flow and any potential short wave\nenergy migration. There still seems to be pretty good confidence \nin keeping any surface fronts north of the Ohio River, allowing \nfor only weak forcing across our area. As such, expect mainly \nafternoon/early evening PoPs in place each day through Sunday, but\nconvection should be more scattered in nature. Highs each day \nwill average in the lower to middle 80s. Lows will moderate \nslightly from the mid to upper 60s to near 70 by next weekend, \nwith elevated humidity levels in place.",
